## Canon Text

### [[Handbook_Rule_01_Data_Bundle]]
> **Rule #1:** Never let a single platform hold more than your next data bundle.
> **Tags:** #rule #liquidity #resilience #early

### [[Handbook_Rule_02_Accumulation]]
> **Rule #2:** Accumulate to dilute. Do not withdraw at minimum threshold when flat fees eat your capital.
> **Tags:** #rule #liquidity #fees #early

### [[Handbook_Rule_03_Blind_Platform]]
> **Rule #3:** The platform isn't malicious. It's blind to the last mile. Your job is to see what it cannot.
> **Tags:** #rule #trust #visibility #early

### [[Handbook_Rule_04_Signal]]
> **Rule #4:** Dollars are not a product. Dollars are a signal — stop chasing capital, build the conditions that attract it.
> **Tags:** #rule #value #trust #early

### [[Handbook_Rule_05_Sentinel]]
> **Rule #5:** Every gate has a sentinel. Every sentinel has incentives. Study the reason the lock was installed — not the lock itself.
> **Tags:** #rule #access #incentives #early

### [[Handbook_Rule_06_Layer]]
> **Rule #6:** Value explains why opportunity appears. Infrastructure determines where it flows. Locate your layer before you build your strategy.
> **Tags:** #rule #infrastructure #positioning #early

### [[Handbook_Rule_07_Borrowed_Ground]]
> **Rule #7:** Borrowed ground should never become permanent residence. The platform may introduce you. Ownership is what allows you to stay.
> **Tags:** #rule #ownership #distribution #early

### [[Handbook_Rule_08_Portability]]
> **Rule #8:** Ownership protects your rights. Portability protects your continuity. An asset that cannot survive migration is not fully owned — it is parked on borrowed ground.
> **Tags:** #rule #ownership #portability #resilience

### [[Handbook_Rule_09_Movement]]
> **Rule #9:** Movement is universal. Infrastructure determines how far value can travel. Ownership determines who benefits when it arrives.
> **Source:** [[Digital_Frontier_Dispatch_008_Distribution_Network]] | Gate 03
> **Tags:** #rule-9 #gate-3 #distribution

### [[Handbook_Rule_10_Protocol]]
> **Rule #10:** Do not optimize for the interface. Map the protocol. The dashboard tells you what happened. The architecture determines what becomes possible.
> **Source:** [[Digital_Frontier_Dispatch_009_Leverage_Protocol]] | Gate 03
> **Tags:** #rule-10 #gate-3 #protocol

### [[Handbook_Rule_11_Six_Layers]]
> **Rule #11:** Plug into the six layers — compute, payments, identity, distribution, trust, data — and effort compounds. Operate outside them, and you burn out against friction you can't see.
> **Source:** [[Digital_Frontier_Dispatch_010_Leverage_Points]] | Gate 04
> **Stack:** `[[Compute]]` | `[[Payments]]` | `[[Identity]]` | `[[Distribution]]` | `[[Trust]]` | `[[Data]]`
> **Tags:** #rule-11 #gate-4 #leverage

### [[Handbook_Rule_12_Control_Layer]] — PENDING
> **Rule #12:** The control layer isn't a person you can appeal to, it is a chain of automated systems, each holding only part of the picture. Therefore: Prepare for the handoff between systems - not just the systems themselves.
> **Source:** [[Digital_Frontier_Dispatch_011_The_Control_Layers]] | Gate 04
> **Switches:** Identity → Liquidity → Clearing → Access
> **Tags:** #rule-12 #gate-4 #control-layer #kenyan-question

